00:10:26.240 --> 00:10:37.170
Cole Medin: And so, the reason we care about building our own second brain in the first place is because of something called the lethal trifecta. I've covered this once before in the community.

52
00:10:37.240 --> 00:10:48.580
Cole Medin: But this is what makes personal agents very dangerous. And so, the lethal trifecta, it applies to more than just second brains, or personal agents. This applies to all agents.

53
00:10:48.870 --> 00:11:05.629
Cole Medin: there are 3 parts to the trifecta here. Obviously, that's why it's called a trifecta. We have private data access, we have untrusted content, so there are other people that are able to give inputs into our agent, and then we have what is called an exfiltration vector.

54
00:11:05.630 --> 00:11:19.429
Cole Medin: An exfiltration vector, it's kind of a fancy phrase. All it means is there's some way for the agent to send information back to a malicious attacker through, you know, making an API request or sending a message on Telegram, something like that.

55
00:11:19.710 --> 00:11:29.940
Cole Medin: The thought process behind the lethal trifecta is your agent is at most risk of being compromised if it meets all three of these criteria.

56
00:11:30.320 --> 00:11:49.890
Cole Medin: So if it can access private data, receive untrusted inputs, and have some way to send information back out. Because think about it this way. If your AI agent is able to access things in your email, you know, access private data, and it can receive incoming emails, so theoretically, there could be a prompt injection attack that comes in through an email.

57
00:11:49.960 --> 00:12:01.739
Cole Medin: But if there's no way for it to send private data back out to the attacker, your agent is a lot harder to compromise if it only matches two out of the three criteria in the lethal trifecta.

58
00:12:01.740 --> 00:12:13.440
Cole Medin: And just to give you another example here, let's say that there is an exfiltration vector. Your agent is allowed to make arbitrary API requests so it can send data out to the internet, and it could theoretically have a prompt injection attack.

59
00:12:13.550 --> 00:12:29.949
Cole Medin: but there's no access to private data, then it doesn't really matter if there is a prompt injection attack, because what is it really going to access, even if it could send something to an attacker? Not that your agent is fully secure at this point, but just saying that, like, we really need to hit on all three of these points.

60
00:12:30.090 --> 00:12:35.040
Cole Medin: In order for there to really be a big, big security concern with our agent.

61
00:12:35.160 --> 00:12:44.049
Cole Medin: And the thing is, in order for a second brain to be useful for us, it really has to have all three of these criteria met by default.

62
00:12:44.370 --> 00:12:54.509
Cole Medin: Like, if there's no exfiltration vector, it means that it's not possible for my agent to make any kind of API request. It can't even build Python scripts for me, like, it'd be very limiting.

63
00:12:54.510 --> 00:13:16.830
Cole Medin: And obviously, we want our agent to deal with our inbox, and deal with, you know, searching the web, so pulling in other information from the web. We want it to help us manage our life, so it's gonna have access to our tasks, and our calendar, and our email, and I'll show you what it looks like to set up all of that. And so, all this means that, like, we have to be very, very careful about bringing an AI agent as a second brain into our life.

